1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,145
In C, you would write a struct.

2
00:00:03,195 --> 00:00:07,110
Python has two ways to do it,
and they are not the same.

3
00:00:07,933 --> 00:00:09,234
First, NamedTuple.

4
00:00:09,284 --> 00:00:12,085
It is a tuple whose fields have names.

5
00:00:12,135 --> 00:00:16,211
You list the fields, and
Python writes the constructor.

6
00:00:16,261 --> 00:00:18,987
Read a field by name, or by position.

7
00:00:19,037 --> 00:00:22,363
But once it is created,
it cannot be changed.

8
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:24,440
Second, @dataclass.

9
00:00:24,490 --> 00:00:29,126
It is an ordinary class, where Python
writes the boilerplate for you:

10
00:00:29,176 --> 00:00:32,793
the constructor, the printout,
and the equality check.

11
00:00:32,843 --> 00:00:34,830
And its fields can be changed.

12
00:00:35,667 --> 00:00:36,814
So compare them.

13
00:00:36,864 --> 00:00:42,352
Assign to a field: the NamedTuple raises
an error, the @dataclass is fine.

14
00:00:42,402 --> 00:00:47,741
Unpack it into two names: the NamedTuple
works, the @dataclass does not.

15
00:00:47,791 --> 00:00:51,857
Use it as a dictionary key:
again, only the NamedTuple.

16
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:57,698
The rule: if it never changes after
it is created, use a NamedTuple.

17
00:00:57,748 --> 00:01:03,155
If its fields get reassigned, use a
@dataclass. A stack entry is the first.

18
00:01:03,205 --> 00:01:08,030
A linked list node, whose next
pointer keeps moving, is the second.
